00:27:10.020 --> 00:27:11.000
I mean, think of Mario Party.

00:27:11.140 --> 00:27:12.820
There's different control schemes in each of those things.

00:27:12.980 --> 00:27:15.200
I think it's just how they're handled.

00:27:15.540 --> 00:27:20.740
I mean, I think the cohesion is hurt because each time I pick up a new game, I'm, like,

00:27:20.840 --> 00:27:22.880
frustrated from scratch again.

00:27:23.480 --> 00:27:24.820
You know, to varying degrees.

00:27:24.940 --> 00:27:26.500
There are some that aren't as frustrating as others.

00:27:26.620 --> 00:27:31.820
But I just, I think that, like, if you're going to have different control schemes for

00:27:31.820 --> 00:27:36.960
five different games, they need to all be good, and they need to all be somewhat intuitive

00:27:36.960 --> 00:27:39.080
or very clearly explained.

00:27:39.080 --> 00:27:41.920
Yeah, I mean, like, they do explain it in the manual.

00:27:42.200 --> 00:27:44.160
I had to open it up for a couple of them.

00:27:46.280 --> 00:27:52.720
But no, like, a game like this has to have variety, or it, like, why make a game about

00:27:52.720 --> 00:27:55.080
a bunch of mini-games if they're all going to control the same?

00:27:56.720 --> 00:28:02.760
Unless, I mean, I could see it being, like, mini-games of goals, you know, where it's like,

00:28:03.000 --> 00:28:04.200
yeah, you just play like Mario.

00:28:04.200 --> 00:28:05.960
But in this one, you've got to collect a bunch of things.

00:28:06.060 --> 00:28:07.360
In this one, you've got to run from something.

00:28:07.480 --> 00:28:09.620
And in this one, it's an obstacle course, or whatever.

00:28:09.780 --> 00:28:10.740
Like, I get that.

00:28:10.920 --> 00:28:12.780
But yeah, I know that's not what they're trying to do.

00:28:23.860 --> 00:28:42.120
I know that's not what they're trying to do.

00:28:54.540 --> 00:28:59.140
Yeah, the note about the manual and learning how to play each of these different control schemes

00:28:59.720 --> 00:29:03.040
brings up a good point about just how they went about doing it in the manual.

00:29:03.040 --> 00:29:08.120
There isn't, like, a simplified version of, like, and here's the controller again, and

00:29:08.120 --> 00:29:09.260
here's what each button does.

00:29:09.260 --> 00:29:14.880
It's like, you really do have to read a lot of text to understand.

00:29:15.060 --> 00:29:19.480
And granted, you know, it's probably the clearest way to make sure everybody gets it.

00:29:19.580 --> 00:29:21.780
But there's no, like, quick reference thing.

00:29:21.960 --> 00:29:24.360
It is like, for instance, fire making.

00:29:24.740 --> 00:29:27.680
Press A button quickly and repeatedly to rub your sticks together.

00:29:27.680 --> 00:29:31.160
The faster you rub, the faster the bar at the bottom of the screen will reach the red zone,

00:29:31.160 --> 00:29:33.880
at which point you'll begin throwing sparks onto the kindling.

00:29:34.100 --> 00:29:38.920
As sparks reach the kindling, press control pad down a few quick times to puff on the

00:29:38.920 --> 00:29:40.280
sparks and help them catch fire.

00:29:40.480 --> 00:29:44.140
You may need to alternate between stick rubs and short, quick puffs to get the kindling

